1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,370
One evening, Asha runs her end-of-day
report.

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:06,230
It prints a total that is much too small.

3
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:08,701
But there is no error, and no crash.

4
00:00:08,721 --> 00:00:10,133
Nothing looks broken.

5
00:00:10,153 --> 00:00:12,383
How do you find a bug that hides?

6
00:00:12,987 --> 00:00:15,272
This is Code Quest, Chapter 9.

7
00:00:15,292 --> 00:00:17,577
Today you become a bug hunter.

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,926
And an AI assistant will promise to stop a
crash.

9
00:00:20,946 --> 00:00:23,658
Its fix will crash on the same division.

10
00:00:24,253 --> 00:00:26,196
So here is today's mission.

11
00:00:26,216 --> 00:00:30,413
Asha's end-of-day report, with three bugs
found and fixed.

12
00:00:30,433 --> 00:00:35,779
When it works, its total matches the money
she took, and she can explain why.

13
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:37,172
Let's start hunting.

14
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:39,930
Tell the three kinds of bugs apart.

15
00:00:39,967 --> 00:00:42,677
Read a traceback, from the bottom up.

16
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:46,220
Find a hidden bug by printing values
inside a loop.

17
00:00:46,267 --> 00:00:50,207
Write a debugging prompt that gives an AI
everything it needs.

18
00:00:50,233 --> 00:00:54,615
And test a fix with several cases,
including the one that failed.

19
00:00:55,217 --> 00:00:59,957
A bug is a mistake that makes a program
stop, or give a wrong result.

20
00:00:59,977 --> 00:01:02,717
Every programmer writes bugs, every day.

21
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:04,483
And here is the AI part.

22
00:01:04,503 --> 00:01:10,487
A vague question, like my code is wrong,
fix it, often gets a confident fix for the

23
00:01:10,507 --> 00:01:11,500
wrong problem.

24
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:16,333
Bugs come in three kinds, and each one
shows itself in a different way.

25
00:01:16,367 --> 00:01:19,418
A syntax error means Python cannot read
the file.

26
00:01:19,438 --> 00:01:22,677
So it runs nothing at all, not even the
first print.

27
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,870
A runtime error happens while the program
runs.

28
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:29,670
Some lines print, then Python stops and
prints a report.

29
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:31,852
A logic error is the sneakiest.

30
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:34,515
The program finishes, with no message.

31
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:36,197
But the result is wrong.

32
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:37,616
Quick check.

33
00:01:37,636 --> 00:01:41,696
A program prints two lines, then stops
with an error report.

34
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:43,328
Which kind of bug is it?

35
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:54,810
A.

36
00:01:54,830 --> 00:01:56,009
A runtime error.

37
00:01:56,029 --> 00:01:59,905
Some lines ran first, so Python could read
the file.

38
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:01,595
Take a breather.

39
00:02:01,615 --> 00:02:03,580
Here is what you know so far.

40
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,836
Next, Asha meets each kind of bug in her
report.

41
00:02:11,583 --> 00:02:12,123
Step 1.

42
00:02:12,143 --> 00:02:15,163
Asha's report starts with three lines.

43
00:02:15,183 --> 00:02:17,243
Line 2 prints the heading.

44
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:18,363
It is correct.

45
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:20,448
Line 4 prints the tea price.

46
00:02:20,468 --> 00:02:23,992
Look at its end: the closing bracket is
missing.

47
00:02:35,347 --> 00:02:35,520
No.

48
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:36,941
Nothing prints at all.

49
00:02:36,961 --> 00:02:42,237
Python reads the whole file before it runs
any line, and it could not read line 4.

50
00:02:42,267 --> 00:02:44,599
The report names the file and line 4.

51
00:02:44,619 --> 00:02:49,177
The marker points at the opening bracket,
the one that was never closed.

52
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,800
The fix goes at the end of the line: a
closing bracket.

53
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:56,242
These messages come from Python 3.12 and
newer.

54
00:02:56,262 --> 00:02:59,524
Older versions word some of them
differently.

55
00:03:00,117 --> 00:03:00,657
Step 2.

56
00:03:00,677 --> 00:03:04,102
Line 3 asks how many cups of tea were
sold.

57
00:03:04,122 --> 00:03:08,027
Remember Chapter 4: input always gives
back text.

58
00:03:08,067 --> 00:03:10,391
Line 4 prints the answer.

59
00:03:10,411 --> 00:03:14,986
Line 5 multiplies the cups by the tea
price, 3.5.

60
00:03:26,347 --> 00:03:30,183
Lines 2, 3 and 4 run, so their output
appears.

61
00:03:30,203 --> 00:03:32,530
Then Python stops at line 5.

62
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,390
This report is called a traceback.

63
00:03:35,410 --> 00:03:37,208
Read it from the bottom up.

64
00:03:37,228 --> 00:03:40,710
The last line names the kind of error, a
Type Error.

65
00:03:40,730 --> 00:03:44,750
A sequence is a value made of parts in
order, like a string.

66
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,771
The two lines above show the code, and the
markers point at the part that failed:

67
00:03:50,791 --> 00:03:51,880
cups times 3.5.

68
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,610
Above that: the file, and line 5.

69
00:03:54,633 --> 00:03:57,851
And the first line says: most recent call
last.

70
00:03:57,871 --> 00:04:00,744
It means the newest step is at the bottom.

71
00:04:00,764 --> 00:04:03,913
In programs this size, there is only one
step.

72
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:08,321
In Chapter 16, when you write functions,
tracebacks grow longer.

73
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:10,437
Here is the key idea.

74
00:04:10,457 --> 00:04:14,390
The line Python names is where it noticed
the problem.

75
00:04:14,410 --> 00:04:16,220
The cause can be earlier.

76
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:19,423
Line 3 stored the answer as text.

77
00:04:19,443 --> 00:04:23,947
So the fix is on line 3: int, around the
input.

78
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:25,770
Now 12 cups cost 42.00.

79
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:26,936
Step 3.

80
00:04:26,956 --> 00:04:29,531
This loop adds up three sales.

81
00:04:29,551 --> 00:04:32,040
Line 2 starts the total at 0.

82
00:04:32,067 --> 00:04:34,121
Line 3 makes three passes.

83
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:37,232
Line 4 asks for the price of each sale.

84
00:04:37,252 --> 00:04:39,227
Line 5 adds to the total.

85
00:04:39,267 --> 00:04:43,676
Before you run any program, work out the
expected result by hand.

86
00:04:43,696 --> 00:04:46,197
Otherwise, a wrong answer looks fine.

87
00:04:57,547 --> 00:04:59,555
It should print 14.00.

88
00:04:59,575 --> 00:05:01,307
But it prints 6.00!

89
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:02,965
No error, no crash.

90
00:05:02,985 --> 00:05:04,703
Only a wrong number.

91
00:05:04,723 --> 00:05:10,179
That is a logic error, and only your
expected result caught it.

92
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:11,177
Step 4.

93
00:05:11,197 --> 00:05:16,282
To find a hidden bug, print the values of
your variables while the program runs.

94
00:05:16,302 --> 00:05:18,260
This is called print debugging.

95
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:20,746
Asha adds line 6 inside the loop.

96
00:05:20,766 --> 00:05:24,109
It shows sale, price and total on every
pass.

97
00:05:24,129 --> 00:05:29,340
It starts with the word DEBUG, so she can
find it and remove it later.

98
00:05:29,367 --> 00:05:34,245
Line 5 runs before the debug line, so you
see the total after it changes.

99
00:05:45,613 --> 00:05:46,768
Total is 1.

100
00:05:46,788 --> 00:05:48,583
It should be 4.5.

101
00:05:48,633 --> 00:05:49,871
Then 3, then 6.

102
00:05:49,891 --> 00:05:55,323
The total grows by the sale number, 1, 2
and 3, not by the price.

103
00:05:55,367 --> 00:05:58,788
So line 5 adds sale, where it should add
price.

104
00:05:59,387 --> 00:06:05,075
Asha changes line 5 to add price, and
removes the debug line.

105
00:06:05,095 --> 00:06:08,818
The total is 14.00, exactly as expected.

106
00:06:09,417 --> 00:06:09,845
Step 5.

107
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:11,702
An AI cannot see your screen.

108
00:06:11,722 --> 00:06:13,174
So give it four things.

109
00:06:13,194 --> 00:06:15,095
The code, and the exact error.

110
00:06:15,115 --> 00:06:17,657
If there is none, say: no error message.

111
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:22,911
Then the expected result, worked out by
hand, and the actual result.

112
00:06:22,931 --> 00:06:25,450
The difference points at the bug.

113
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,879
Then ask for the cause before the fix.

114
00:06:27,899 --> 00:06:30,719
A cause you understand teaches you
something.

115
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:32,086
Quick check.

116
00:06:32,106 --> 00:06:35,231
Your program prints a wrong total, and no
error.

117
00:06:35,251 --> 00:06:37,721
Which parts of the prompt matter most?

118
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:49,200
B.

119
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:52,145
The expected result and the actual result.

120
00:06:52,165 --> 00:06:56,141
With no error message, their difference is
the only clue.

121
00:06:56,753 --> 00:06:57,321
Breather.

122
00:06:57,341 --> 00:07:00,259
You have found and fixed three kinds of
bugs.

123
00:07:00,279 --> 00:07:01,695
Now, the whole report.

124
00:07:06,417 --> 00:07:07,720
Back to the mission.

125
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:11,359
Line 2 prints the heading, and every
bracket is closed.

126
00:07:11,379 --> 00:07:15,727
Line 3 converts the number of sales with
int, the fix from step 2.

127
00:07:15,767 --> 00:07:17,997
Line 4 starts the total at 0.

128
00:07:18,017 --> 00:07:22,033
Lines 5 to 7 add up each price: the fix
from step 4.

129
00:07:22,053 --> 00:07:26,767
Range goes up to sales plus 1, so it asks
once for each sale.

130
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:32,705
Line 8 divides the total by the number of
sales, and lines 9 to 11 print the report,

131
00:07:32,725 --> 00:07:34,398
with two decimal places.

132
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:46,957
Average, 4.67.

133
00:07:46,977 --> 00:07:53,370
14 divided by 3 is 4.666 and more, and two
decimal places round it to 4.67.

134
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:57,119
And the total is 14.00, the money Asha
took.

135
00:07:57,139 --> 00:07:58,564
Mission complete!

136
00:07:58,584 --> 00:08:01,538
But Asha has one more test in mind.

137
00:08:01,558 --> 00:08:05,192
What happens on a day with no sales at
all?

138
00:08:05,212 --> 00:08:10,376
She will find out, with an AI assistant,
one stage at a time.

139
00:08:10,950 --> 00:08:16,440
This chapter's AI skill is the debugging
prompt: the code, the exact error, the

140
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:19,160
expected result, and the actual result.

141
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:21,027
Ask for the cause first.

142
00:08:21,047 --> 00:08:23,258
Then test every fix yourself.

143
00:08:23,278 --> 00:08:27,029
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

144
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,628
Stage 1: plan.

145
00:08:28,648 --> 00:08:31,785
Asha types 0 sales, and the report
crashes.

146
00:08:31,805 --> 00:08:35,749
Before any fix, she asks for a plan to
find the cause.

147
00:08:35,769 --> 00:08:36,630
No code yet.

148
00:08:36,667 --> 00:08:38,418
The AI gives five steps.

149
00:08:38,438 --> 00:08:41,592
Copy the traceback, and read its last
line.

150
00:08:41,612 --> 00:08:43,732
Find the line and its values.

151
00:08:43,752 --> 00:08:46,167
Decide what 0 sales should print.

152
00:08:46,187 --> 00:08:49,046
Then test 0, and the cases that worked.

153
00:08:49,653 --> 00:08:50,744
Stage 2: write.

154
00:08:50,764 --> 00:08:52,372
Asha follows the plan.

155
00:08:52,392 --> 00:08:56,813
She runs the report with 0, and reads the
traceback herself.

156
00:08:56,833 --> 00:09:01,656
She writes down her reading: a Zero
Division Error, on line 8, because sales

157
00:09:01,676 --> 00:09:01,974
is 0.

158
00:09:01,994 --> 00:09:06,243
Then she asks the AI to check her reading,
without fixing anything.

159
00:09:06,267 --> 00:09:09,085
The AI agrees with all three points.

160
00:09:09,105 --> 00:09:12,869
Dividing by zero has no answer, so Python
stops.

161
00:09:13,487 --> 00:09:14,894
Stage 3: generate.

162
00:09:14,914 --> 00:09:19,808
Asha asks for a check, so that 0 sales
prints, No sales today.

163
00:09:19,828 --> 00:09:22,187
She asks what it prints for 0.

164
00:09:22,233 --> 00:09:27,148
The AI adds an if before the average, and
promises: no sales today.

165
00:09:27,168 --> 00:09:29,063
It sounds completely sure.

166
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:33,341
Look at line 8, and remember the boundary
values from Chapter 6.

167
00:09:44,713 --> 00:09:44,925
No.

168
00:09:44,945 --> 00:09:48,869
Greater than or equal to 0 is true when
sales is 0.

169
00:09:48,889 --> 00:09:52,116
So the program still reaches the division.

170
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:54,332
Asha runs it with 0.

171
00:09:54,352 --> 00:09:56,781
The same crash, now on line 9.

172
00:09:56,801 --> 00:10:00,209
The AI's fix crashed on the same division.

173
00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:01,986
Stage 4: test.

174
00:10:02,006 --> 00:10:08,597
Asha asks for test cases, each with the
exact input and the expected result: 0

175
00:10:08,617 --> 00:10:10,800
sales, 1 sale and 3 sales.

176
00:10:10,833 --> 00:10:12,871
The AI lists three cases.

177
00:10:12,891 --> 00:10:18,879
1 sale and 0 sales are edge cases: unusual
answers that often break code.

178
00:10:19,487 --> 00:10:23,722
She runs every case on the AI's code.

179
00:10:23,742 --> 00:10:28,322
Three sales give 14.00 and 4.67, a pass.

180
00:10:28,342 --> 00:10:31,542
One sale of 5.00 passes too.

181
00:10:32,153 --> 00:10:33,669
So two cases pass.

182
00:10:33,689 --> 00:10:37,253
But the case that matters, 0 sales, fails.

183
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:40,877
The AI's code only works when there was
nothing to fix.

184
00:10:41,487 --> 00:10:42,545
Stage 5: debug.

185
00:10:42,565 --> 00:10:48,442
Now Asha writes the full debugging prompt:
the code, the exact error, the expected

186
00:10:48,462 --> 00:10:52,397
result, no sales today, and the actual
result, a crash.

187
00:10:52,433 --> 00:10:54,688
She asks for the cause first.

188
00:10:54,708 --> 00:10:59,239
The AI names line 8: greater than or equal
lets 0 through.

189
00:10:59,259 --> 00:11:01,984
The smallest fix is greater than 0.

190
00:11:02,587 --> 00:11:06,010
Asha changes line 8 and runs it with 0.

191
00:11:06,030 --> 00:11:07,335
No sales today.

192
00:11:07,355 --> 00:11:08,130
No crash.

193
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:12,751
A fix can break a case that worked before.

194
00:11:12,771 --> 00:11:15,548
So she runs every case again.

195
00:11:15,568 --> 00:11:21,818
Three sales: still 14.00 and 4.67, and one
sale still gives 5.00.

196
00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:25,118
All three cases pass on the fixed code.

197
00:11:25,720 --> 00:11:26,885
Stage 6: improve.

198
00:11:26,905 --> 00:11:28,071
The report works.

199
00:11:28,091 --> 00:11:33,022
Asha asks for at most two changes that
make future bugs easier to find.

200
00:11:33,042 --> 00:11:36,020
And she adds: do not change what it
prints.

201
00:11:36,067 --> 00:11:41,165
Suggestion 1: rename sale to sale number,
so nobody adds it instead of price.

202
00:11:41,185 --> 00:11:44,887
That would have made the bug from step 4
easier to spot!

203
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:50,500
Suggestion 2: keep a debug line inside the
loop, so the total is always visible.

204
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,498
Asha tries both suggestions.

205
00:11:53,518 --> 00:11:55,640
Line 5 uses the new name.

206
00:11:55,667 --> 00:11:59,598
And line 8 is the debug line the AI wants
to keep.

207
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:11,192
No.

208
00:12:11,212 --> 00:12:14,678
Three extra debug lines appear in the
report.

209
00:12:14,698 --> 00:12:17,080
The AI ignored her instruction.

210
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:21,675
Debug lines are for finding a bug, not for
the finished report.

211
00:12:21,695 --> 00:12:24,009
So Asha keeps suggestion 1 only.

212
00:12:24,620 --> 00:12:30,206
Here is the result: a clear name on line
5, the check on line 8, and the same

213
00:12:30,226 --> 00:12:31,444
report as before.

214
00:12:31,464 --> 00:12:35,667
Every stage helped, and Asha checked every
answer herself.

215
00:12:36,287 --> 00:12:37,144
Quick check.

216
00:12:37,164 --> 00:12:40,436
An AI fixes your code, and says it works
now.

217
00:12:40,456 --> 00:12:41,899
What do you do next?

218
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:53,400
D.

219
00:12:53,420 --> 00:12:57,041
Run the case that failed, and the cases
that worked.

220
00:12:57,061 --> 00:12:59,212
A fix can break something else.

221
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:00,469
Breather.

222
00:13:00,489 --> 00:13:06,486
Asha caught two AI mistakes: a check that
let 0 through, and a change she did not

223
00:13:06,506 --> 00:13:07,080
ask for.

224
00:13:07,100 --> 00:13:10,360
Next, mistakes beginners make while
debugging.

225
00:13:15,083 --> 00:13:15,926
Now you try.

226
00:13:15,946 --> 00:13:20,095
Line 3 should print the word Total and the
value of total.

227
00:13:31,447 --> 00:13:33,551
A comma between the two values.

228
00:13:33,571 --> 00:13:37,047
And look at the last line: Python even
suggests it.

229
00:13:37,067 --> 00:13:40,939
Python's suggestions are often right, so
always read them.

230
00:13:41,553 --> 00:13:42,269
One more.

231
00:13:42,289 --> 00:13:44,968
Asha adds a debug line on line 5.

232
00:13:44,988 --> 00:13:47,421
Compare its start with line 4.

233
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:03,266
Line 5 has six spaces at the start, but
the block uses four.

234
00:14:03,286 --> 00:14:05,970
Python reports an Indentation Error.

235
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,439
The fix: indent a debug line exactly like
the line above it.

236
00:14:11,053 --> 00:14:11,775
Mistake 3.

237
00:14:11,795 --> 00:14:16,370
In many countries, people write four and a
half as 4 comma 50.

238
00:14:16,390 --> 00:14:21,633
Python accepts only a decimal point, so
float stops with a Value Error.

239
00:14:21,667 --> 00:14:25,769
The message even shows the exact text that
failed.

240
00:14:25,789 --> 00:14:27,830
The fix: type 4 point 50.

241
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:29,337
Last breather.

242
00:14:29,357 --> 00:14:32,950
Now you know three mistakes that slow down
a bug hunt.

243
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,319
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

244
00:14:41,050 --> 00:14:41,434
First.

245
00:14:41,454 --> 00:14:46,411
A program gives the right total for 1
sale, but a wrong total for 3 sales.

246
00:14:46,431 --> 00:14:50,783
What kind of bug is it, and what would you
print inside the loop?

247
00:15:02,147 --> 00:15:05,086
Think about which values change on every
pass.

248
00:15:05,687 --> 00:15:06,129
Second.

249
00:15:06,149 --> 00:15:11,149
What happens if someone types a negative
number of sales into Asha's report?

250
00:15:11,169 --> 00:15:12,933
Predict first, then run it.

251
00:15:24,313 --> 00:15:27,608
Then decide what the report should print
in that case.

252
00:15:28,183 --> 00:15:28,962
Let's recap.

253
00:15:28,982 --> 00:15:31,093
A syntax error stops everything.

254
00:15:31,113 --> 00:15:33,091
A runtime error stops partway.

255
00:15:33,111 --> 00:15:36,553
A logic error gives a wrong result, with
no message.

256
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:38,763
Read a traceback from the bottom up.

257
00:15:38,783 --> 00:15:43,130
The line Python names is where it noticed,
and the cause can be earlier.

258
00:15:43,167 --> 00:15:46,380
Print debugging shows values while the
program runs.

259
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:51,416
And a debugging prompt has the code, the
exact error, the expected result and the

260
00:15:51,436 --> 00:15:52,287
actual result.

261
00:15:52,333 --> 00:15:56,777
And after a fix, test the case that
failed, and the cases that worked.

262
00:15:57,387 --> 00:15:58,444
One last check.

263
00:15:58,464 --> 00:16:01,676
A program stops on line 12 with a Type
Error.

264
00:16:01,696 --> 00:16:03,328
Where can the cause be?

265
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:14,814
C.

266
00:16:14,834 --> 00:16:19,503
On line 12, or on an earlier line, like
the cups from step 2.

267
00:16:20,120 --> 00:16:21,439
Mission complete!

268
00:16:21,459 --> 00:16:24,825
You earned 100 XP and the Bug Hunter
badge.

269
00:16:27,050 --> 00:16:28,621
Now it is your turn.

270
00:16:28,641 --> 00:16:32,440
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

271
00:16:32,467 --> 00:16:37,407
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each,
including reading a traceback.

272
00:16:37,433 --> 00:16:39,728
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each.

273
00:16:39,748 --> 00:16:42,673
In one, a wrong total looks right by luck.

274
00:16:42,700 --> 00:16:48,247
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each: a
report with three bugs, and an AI's member

275
00:16:48,267 --> 00:16:49,130
price to fix.

276
00:16:49,167 --> 00:16:52,371
Work out the expected result before you
run anything.

277
00:16:52,391 --> 00:16:54,987
Try each task before you open the
solution.

278
00:16:55,583 --> 00:16:58,743
Today Asha's report learned to handle 0
sales.

279
00:16:58,763 --> 00:17:01,025
But it still trusts every answer.

280
00:17:01,045 --> 00:17:04,343
Type a word instead of a number, and it
crashes.

281
00:17:04,367 --> 00:17:09,264
In Chapter 10, the second boss level, you
will build a number guessing game.

282
00:17:09,284 --> 00:17:14,247
A secret number, hints, a limit on tries,
and answers that are checked first.

283
00:17:14,267 --> 00:17:15,926
Do the warm-up tasks now.

284
00:17:15,946 --> 00:17:17,807
They take about ten minutes.

285
00:17:17,833 --> 00:17:19,509
See you in Chapter 10.
